body:
There's currently no mechanism for specifying the order of ruby text when it differs from the order of base text. This will be needed to make a(n unimplemented) feature like CSS `ruby-merge: collapse` work in common cases such as names. Example:

````
<ruby xml:lang="en-latn">
    <abbr>H</abbr>
    <rt xml:lang="zh-hant">火</rt>
    <abbr>T</abbr>
    <rt xml:lang="zh-hant">土</rt>
    <span xml:space="preserve"> </span>
    <rt/>
    Cho
    <rt xml:lang="zh-hant">卓</rt>
</ruby>
````

If you were to collapse the preceding ruby text, the name would read in source order, which is wrong; the last name should be first in Chinese. If we consider a case from an English perspective, my name might become "Dark Patrick", which is likewise wrong.

Providing something like a `merge-order` attribute on `rt` elements will not only allow specifying this missing semantic data, but can be immediately put to use. Today, I can do this (with `data-*` attributes):

````
ruby {
	display: inline-flex;
}
ruby * {
	all: unset;
}
ruby > :not(rb):not(rp):not(ruby) {
	white-space: pre;
}
rt[data-merge-order="1"] {
	order: 1;
}
rt[data-merge-order="2"] {
	order: 2;
}
rt[data-merge-order="3"] {
	order: 3;
}
rt[data-merge-order="1"]::before {
	content: " (";
}
ruby::after {
	content: ")";
	order: 100;
}
````

This allows one to inline ruby text which is key to making it usable in prose. In this case, the text would read "HT Cho (卓火土)" given the appropriate merge order attributes.

The CSS becomes even cleaner given the (unimplemented) CSS3 variant of the `attr` function:

````
ruby {
	display: inline-flex;
}
ruby * {
	all: unset;
}
ruby > :not(rb):not(rp):not(ruby) {
	white-space: pre;
}
rt {
	order: attr(data-merge-order number);
}
rt[data-merge-order="1"]::before {
	content: " (";
}
ruby::after {
	content: ")";
	order: 100;
}
````
